<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edward the Confessor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1003–1066</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Edward the Confessor was king of the English from 1042 to 1066, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelred-the-unready">Æthelred the Unready</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emma-of-normandy">Emma of Normandy</a>. Raised in exile in Normandy during the Danish rule of Cnut and his sons, he succeeded his half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harthacnut">Harthacnut</a>. He built the first great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-abbey">Westminster Abbey</a> and was later canonised. His death without children led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norman-conquest">Norman Conquest</a>, as both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harold-godwinson">Harold Godwinson</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William of Normandy</a> claimed the throne.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edward-the-confessor.jpg" alt="Edward the Confessor" width="320" height="351" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Myrabella · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bayeux_Tapestry_scene1_EDWARD_REX.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
